#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161968 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161760 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85e1fe3f-e85e-5ab4-a594-c55b84bb789a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "f9912357-1d1d-5413-917e-257dc5dfc0fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8051" :question/problem "![](images/94/941de9bd4837b4b5c9f0d2bf64c4ea0fd9be657b0368d03cac169742083822d1.png)\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_3,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of $a_{13}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}: &={C}_{1}+6{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}: &={C}_{2}+5{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -16 & -17 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $C_1:=C_1+6C_3\\quad$ and $\\quad C_2:=C_2+5C_3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85e1fe3f-e85e-5ab4-a594-c55b84bb789a" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527289 :question-generator/id #uuid "49438a09-d1b7-527f-822e-2fcd2c7a1dcf" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186161968.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85e1fe3f-e85e-5ab4-a594-c55b84bb789a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:an